The plateau is not perfectly steady. Figure 1b shows a 1 s detail: the force oscillates coherently about its mean with a period of about 0.106 s, i.e. a frequency of about 9.4 Hz. The rms fluctuation is 4.6% of the mean for this run. The oscillation is discussed further in §3.3. Across the 29 accepted runs, the mean force follows a power law $F \propto U^{n}$ with $n = 2.01$ overall (2.00–2.04 for the individual cylinders), consistent with quadratic drag and with the quality of the force measurements.

![**Figure 1.** (a) Streamwise force record for run 5 (1 in cylinder, $U = 0.6$ m s$^{-1}$). The shaded region marks the steady window $3 \le t \le 8$ s. (b) Detail of the steady window. The oscillation is at approximately 9.4 Hz, twice the vortex-shedding frequency.](figures/fig1_timeseries.png){width=5.2in}

### 3.2 Mean drag coefficient

with a standard error of the mean of 0.006 (0.6%). The per-cylinder means are $1.143 \pm 0.033$ (1 in), $1.152 \pm 0.026$ (1.5 in), and $1.157 \pm 0.034$ (2 in), where the uncertainties are one standard deviation. The largest difference between cylinder means is 0.014 (1.2%), which is smaller than the run-to-run scatter and is not statistically resolvable.

![**Figure 2.** Drag coefficient versus Reynolds number for the three cylinders. Error bars show the standard error of each run mean; the solid line and shaded band show the mean and $\pm1$ standard deviation of all 29 accepted runs. Run 17 is excluded (see §2.4).](figures/fig2_cd_vs_re.png){width=4.6in}

At matched Reynolds number the comparison is more direct.

The rms fluctuation level, $\sigma_F/F_{\mathrm{mean}}$, is 4.8% on average, with a range of 4.4–6.6% and no clear trend with $Re$ or diameter. The spectra also contain a broadband floor at low $fD/U$, roughly one to two orders of magnitude below the peak, which is likely due to residual facility vibration and background turbulence. The peak remains well resolved above this floor.

![**Figure 3.** Normalized spectra of the streamwise force fluctuation for three runs at matched $Re \approx 2.6\times10^{4}$. The peak at $fD/U = 0.40$ corresponds to twice the vortex-shedding frequency, i.e. $St \approx 0.20$.](figures/fig3_spectra.png){width=4.6in}

### 3.4 Uncertainty and sensitivity

